cm2: an address literal resolves in its own family only, never through DNS

opensync-lab / opensync-rpi: cm2_resolve() asks c-ares for both families when the uplink
has both, and TRY_RESOLVE waits until neither query is in progress. c-ares (1.18.1, the
pod's) answers an address literal at once only in its own family: asked for 10.0.0.1 as
AF_INET6, it sends the name to DNS as a AAAA query, and only after every DNS timeout
falls back to the IPv4 address. So once the uplink had an IPv6 address, an IPv4
redirector or manager literal took as long as DNS took to fail: in an RDK lab
qualification (2026-10-18 17:42 PDT [00:42 UTC]) br-home got a global IPv6 address 9 s
before the resolve, the IPv4 result was there at once ("Resolved state: ipv4: 3 ipv6: 1"),
and the AF_INET6 query of 10.101.0.40, then of 10.0.0.1, ran out TRY_RESOLVE's 180 s
three times ("got address of host 10.0.0.1, req_type: AF_INET6, h addr type = AF_INET"
at each timeout); the agent showed the pod unbound for 9 minutes, until FAST_RECONNECT's
cached address connected. Where DNS answers, the AAAA query fails fast, which is why it
showed up only now and then (a race with the uplink's IPv6). The lab's DNS servers are
unreachable, and the same 180 s timeouts failed RDK lab qualifications on 18, 22 and 28
October, each time after a pod's backhaul move or restart.

An address literal is now asked for in its own family only; a name is asked for as
before. A literal of a family the uplink lacks is asked for in neither, and fails as an
unresolvable name does. The choice is in cm2_resolve_family.h, free of cm2's other state,
so that pod/opensync/tests/cm2_resolve_family_test.c tests it on its own.

Upstream: an OpenSync core behaviour as released in 6.6.1.0: cm2_resolve() in
src/cm2/src/cm2_resolve_ares.c asks every family the uplink has for any hostname. Not
reported (the owner decides).

